WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Why do some animals advertise themselves with bold patterns or dazzling colors?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:17.000
I've been lucky enough one way or another to meet some of our planet's most enchanting animals,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
but some I find particularly intriguing.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000
We've known about some of these creatures for centuries,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
others we've discovered more recently.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
In this series, I share their stories and reveal why they really are natural curiosities.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
The natural world is full of colors and patterns.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
For most of us, many animals are simply beautiful.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Sometimes so beautiful, they become highly collectible.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
But what role do colors and patterns play in the lives of the animals that display them?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The zebra has stripes unlike any other mammal.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
When they first became widely known outside Africa during the 18th century,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
they were much prized pets in menageries of the nobility and royalty.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.000
But while they fascinated the public, they baffled scientists.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.000
What on earth could be the function of these extraordinary stripes?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Well, the answers that have been suggested over the centuries have been truly astounding.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
To begin with, all that was known about zebras was that they lived in herds on the vast plains of Africa.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
But as zebras became more familiar to European society, we began to learn more.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
One particular zebra became something of a celebrity in Georgian England,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.000
so much so that it was painted by the famous racehorse painter George Stubbs.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
This is a copy of his picture.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
It was a belated wedding present to Queen Charlotte from her husband King George III,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
and it actually lived in the grounds of Buckingham House, as Buckingham Palace was then known.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Queen Charlotte was a passionate collector of exotic animals,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
and the zebra soon became the most famous animal in her collection, attracting crowds of visitors.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
George and London was not unfamiliar with bizarre and exotic creatures.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Many curiosities were being sent back from the expanding British Empire,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
but the strange striped horse was particularly intriguing.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Early zebra collectors, in trying to tame them, stumbled upon one possible reason for their stripes.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Queen Charlotte wasn't the only famous European to be fanatical about zebras.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:12.000
So was this gentleman, Lord Clive of India, the British army officer who established British interest there in the 18th century.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:21.000
He actually owned two, a male and a female, and so keen was he to try and get a zebra that would be tameable,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.000
he tried to mate his female zebra with a male donkey.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:32.000
And to make the male donkey more attractive to the female, believe it or not, he painted it with stripes.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:39.000
And the experiment was a success. In due course, the female zebra produced a foal.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
As this old newsreel shows, the offspring of such unions are partially striped.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.000
They're also sometimes fertile.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Lord Clive's success in producing one might suggest that the stripes are indeed important in making one partner attractive to the other.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Charles Darwin built on that idea. He noticed that each individual zebra had its own unique striped pattern.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
He suggested that the stripes were a way for individuals to recognise each other during courtship.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Occasionally, however, a zebra appeared with a coat pattern so odd that it challenged that explanation.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
In 1968, a picture of a very strange zebra appeared in the press.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
It's fair to say that this animal is dotted rather than striped.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.000
And that could give us an insight into the function of its coat patterns.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.000
If it's to do with social cohesion, then you would expect that such a strange creature would be shunned by the rest of the herd.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.000
But that was not so. It was treated just like any other member.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:57.000
So maybe its coat patterns are not primarily to do with social cohesion.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Other theories suggest the stripes play an important role in defence against predators. But how?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:15.000
It's been claimed that the moving striped bodies of a herd of zebras confuse a lion, making it difficult for it to judge distance and so time its pounce.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Others have argued that the stripes break up the zebra's outlines so that they're hard to spot, especially among vegetation.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:34.000
However, research comparing the zebra and the tiger concluded that while a tiger's stripes make it blend with its background, at least our eyes,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.000
the regular spacing of the zebra's stripes actually make it more conspicuous.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:51.000
The bold stripes of Queen Charlotte's zebra drew huge crowds to Buckingham Palace, but the animal itself was proving to be quite a handful.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:00.000
The Queen's lone pet was a somewhat temperamental animal, and its keepers had to warn spectators that it was likely to kick and bite.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.000
And that's hardly surprising, bearing in mind the strange food it was given, which was a mixture of raw meat and tobacco.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Hardly the sort of thing to give to a grazing animal.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:20.000
It also became a way of lampooning the royal family. The animal itself was known as the Queen's ass,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and its stripes were used to indicate the King and Queen's son, Prince George.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:37.000
The Victorians continued the Georgian obsession with taming zebras, but they had a practical reason for doing so.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:44.000
A reason that may provide another explanation for the stripes. Flies.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Flies carry fatal diseases that affect both humans and cattle, and one of the most dangerous in Africa is the tetsy fly.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:00.000
It spreads a disease called sleeping sickness that kills people, cattle and horses.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Early Victorian settlers noticed that while their domestic horses fell ill from sleeping sickness, zebras were not affected.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.000
So they set about taming them.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:23.000
Over the years, many people have attempted to tame zebras, but the efforts of one Victorian eccentric were particularly spectacular.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:30.000
These zebras were once part of a menagerie of a Victorian aristocrat who became obsessed with taming them.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
Otto Rothschild was a member of the great Rothschild banking family, but he wasn't much good as a banker.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:55.000
His main passion was for wildlife, and he had a particular fondness of zebras and spent a great deal of time and effort training them.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Rothschild succeeded where others failed.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:06.000
This extraordinary photograph shows him on a journey to Buckingham Palace with his carriage being drawn by tame zebras.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.000
It was a time-consuming process. He trained each zebra individually using a small carriage.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:22.000
And they didn't take easily to being bridled, but eventually all three of his zebras and a pony pulled his carriage all the way through London to Buckingham Palace.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.000
It must have been a strange spectacle for anybody passing by, but perhaps they didn't notice the zebras.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Walter's brother Charles remarked that the stripes of the zebra made them almost disappear as they travelled through the city streets.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:47.000
Despite Rothschild's efforts, zebras never really became an alternative to the horse in England or in Africa.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:56.000
The observation by those early settlers that zebras seemed immune to the bites of tetsaflies was not entirely correct.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Zebras can be bitten by flies and can suffer the same sickness as the domestic horse.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
But nonetheless, evidence suggests they attract fewer flies.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:18.000
Some scientists have theorised that the zebra stripes may in some way make it more difficult for biting flies to land on a zebra's body.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:25.000
To test this theory, a number of Hungarian scientists took four horse-shaped models.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:34.000
One they painted black, another they painted brown, the third was white and the fourth was painted with the stripes of a zebra.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:41.000
Then they put these four models in a field and covered them with sticky glue.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:48.000
Then after a certain length of time they went and counted the number of flies that had landed on the different bodies.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.000
And believe it or not, there were fewest flies on the zebra's body.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.000
How could this be?

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Well, an insect's eyes are compound, they have a lot of elements in them and they navigate using horizontally polarised light.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:18.000
And it may be that the stripes of the zebra in some way disrupt that polarised light and make it much more difficult for the flies to land on the zebra's body.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:26.000
These recent findings do not prove definitively why zebras got their stripes. There could be several other benefits.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:34.000
But they do suggest that the stripes are more about avoiding being bitten rather than avoiding being eaten.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:40.000
Whatever the biological reason for its stripes, zebras have fascinated us for centuries.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.000
Queen Charlotte was so besotted by hers that eventually she bought another.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:52.000
The first had proved to be so ill-tempered that she sold it to a friend of King George III.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:11:02.000
From there it went to a travelling menagerie and when it died its body was stuffed and put on display in the Blue Boar Inn in York.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.000
Something of a come down from the grounds of Buckingham House.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:18.000
Coming up, we will discover the story behind the pattern of a much more fragile animal as we look closely at the butterfly's wing.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:26.000
Despite the occasional hiccup in its coat markings, the zebra has taken a basic striped pattern and stuck with it.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:34.000
Our second patterned animal has done quite the opposite and has produced thousands, if not millions, of variations on a theme.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:41.000
Victorian naturalists seem to have been obsessed with butterflies.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.000
Their assembled specimens fill the cabinets of many a museum.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:51.000
London's Natural History Museum has over three million of them alone.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
And it's easy to see why those naturalists were so obsessed.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:59.000
Butterflies are so astonishingly varied and stunningly beautiful.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:09.000
When it comes to pattern, nature seems to have excelled herself with the butterfly wing.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Why nature has refashioned the wing into so many different patterns has long fascinated science.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:29.000
The vast majority of the specimens here come from the Victorian era, a period when a passion for amateur collecting reached its peak.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:39.000
Many of those early collectors recognised the relationship between the colour and pattern of a butterfly's wing and its identity as a species.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Each species has its own signature pattern and hue.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
Magnifying a wing shows how these patterns are created.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:54.000
The surface is covered with millions of tiny scales.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:00.000
They're made of chitin and contain different pigments that tend to fade.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:08.000
But there is another kind of wing colouration that gives some butterflies a particular spectacular brilliance.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:12.000
And this remains long after the butterfly is dead.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:17.000
These morpho butterflies were collected over a hundred years ago.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:23.000
And they are as bright today as the day on which they were collected.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:31.000
That is because their wings contain tiny microscopic structures called gyroids.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:36.000
When the light hits one of them, it is bent and refracted.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:43.000
So that the colour it produces varies according to the angle on which you look at it.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:52.000
The gyroid, in fact, is not a pigment which would fade. It's a crystal structure.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Darwin pondered on the reason for such bright colours.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:02.000
They could, after all, make the butterfly highly visible to predators. So why be so colourful?

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:09.000
Victorian naturalists were aware that male and female butterflies of the same species could be very different in colour.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:16.000
The male large blue is indeed blue. But the female, on the other hand, is a drab brown.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:22.000
For Darwin, such species were a perfect example of a process he called sexual selection.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:28.000
A colour or pattern arises among males that is attractive to the opposite sex.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:34.000
So the most brightly coloured male is more likely to get a mate.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:47.000
Remarkably, it's sometimes possible to see the male colouration and female colouration in a single individual butterfly, like this one.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:56.000
That side is female and that's male. Such individuals are called gynandromorphs and they're extremely rare.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:09.000
They're also infertile. But nonetheless, they can reveal a great deal about sex and colouration in butterflies.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:21.000
Studying the genetics of gynandromorphs has enabled scientists to understand the role male and female genes play in the development of wing colour and shape.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:29.000
But why should it be the females who are drab and the males more colourful?

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:39.000
That's because in such species, males are territorial and bright colours visible from a distance keep other males away.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:48.000
The females, on the other hand, are egg layers and it's often better for them to be less conspicuous.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:57.000
But not all butterflies show such clear differences between the sexes. I once visited the winter home of the monarch butterfly in Mexico.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:06.000
Here, tens of millions of butterflies, having left North America to escape the winter, cluster together on trees.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:14.000
Males and females are hardly any different. Both are bright orange with black stripes.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:23.000
It's a magnificent spectacle and one might think that with all these butterflies in one place, they would be a feast for predators.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:31.000
But remarkably, few animals are able to eat monarch butterflies because they are poisonous.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:41.000
Today, our understanding of wing pattern and colour as a means of defence is largely due to the work of one of those impressive Victorian butterfly collectors.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:51.000
In 1848, a young British naturalist called Henry Bates began a collecting expedition to the Amazon that would continue for 11 years.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:17:00.000
Bates was of humble origin and largely self-educated, so he was rather different from other scholars of the time.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:12.000
He travelled to the Amazon with fellow naturalist Alfred Ruffsell Wallace, who wrote that the aim of their journey wasn't just to collect, but to gather facts towards solving the problem of the origin of species.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:21.000
He stayed on the Amazon for more than a decade and amassed thousands of specimens, as well as discovering more than 100 new species of butterfly.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:35.000
His work wasn't just an insight into the diversity of life in the tropics, it was his theory on butterfly colouration that would bring him to the attention of the great Charles Darwin.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:43.000
He recorded differences in butterfly behaviour. Some species flew in a purposeful way.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:52.000
Others were slow and fluttery fliers, yet they were left largely alone despite their conspicuous markings.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:57.000
Bates was aware that some of the butterflies in his collection were distasteful to predators.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:06.000
He knew from his time in the Amazon that some species, like these on the left, avoid predation because they contain poisons.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:15.000
But here was a butterfly that was almost identical, but not quite. In fact, it belongs to a totally different group.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:19.000
As he worked through his huge collection, he began to see a trend.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:30.000
Each poisonous or distasteful species of butterfly had a matching copycat, and he drew them side by side in his book.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.000
He called these copycats mimics.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:43.000
Bates had stumbled upon a different reason for butterfly patterns, one where colours are a warning sign of danger to would-be predators.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:50.000
Those mimics with a similar wing pattern to the distasteful species were more likely to be avoided by predators.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Those that were less convincing were more likely to be killed.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:19:03.000
So, over time, evolution causes these copycats to be almost identical in pattern and colour to the model they mimic.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Darwin was delighted with Bates' observations.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:12.000
The butterfly wing pattern fitted nicely into his new theory of evolution.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:19.000
Bates also discovered that the wing pattern of a butterfly could vary over distance.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:26.000
This is a butterfly called Heliconius, and this is what it looks like in the south of the Amazon Basin.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.000
But this is what it looks like in the north.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:38.000
But even more remarkably, he also discovered that the mimic varies in the same sort of way.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:48.000
That is what the mimic looks like in the south, and this is what it looks like in the north.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:57.000
While Bates explained the importance of wing pattern in antipredation, there was one question he was never able to answer.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:02.000
How did the mimic avoid mating with the model?

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:08.000
After all, they're almost identical, to our eyes at any rate.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:18.000
We now know that many butterflies can see a much broader band of the light spectrum, even the ultraviolet end.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:23.000
This Heliconius butterfly on the left is closely matched by its mimic on the right.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:32.000
To our eyes, they look very similar, but view them in ultraviolet, and we can see that now the mimic is more drab and darker.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:40.000
So butterflies themselves can see the difference more easily than we can.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:48.000
The evolution of wing pattern in butterflies is clearly more complex than those early Victorian collectors could have imagined.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:56.000
Indeed, many different factors may play a role in shaping the colour and pattern of each species' wing.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:02.000
But we have Henry Bates to thank for revealing the connection between colour and defence.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:10.000
When Henry Bates returned from the Amazon, he described his 11 years in the tropics as the best of his life.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:16.000
He had spent the remainder of his career working as assistant secretary to the Royal Geographical Society,

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:20.000
a job that really didn't stretch his amazing scientific mind.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:27.000
Many collectors have contributed butterfly specimens to this impressive collection in the Natural History Museum,

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.000
but thanks to Bates, we're able to see beyond the dazzling variety of wing colours

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:38.000
and find the evolutionary connection between the many different patterns on the butterfly wing.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:46.000
So the striped coat of the zebra and the colourful markings of a butterfly's wing may play similar roles,

00:21:46.000 --> 00:22:09.000
helping protect the animals they decorate.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.000
you
